// @test: typecheck-pass
// @level: L0
// @tier: 0
// @tags: verification, refinement, soundness, smt
// @expected-warning: W0500
// @expected-warning-count: 1
//
// Pin (T0788) — a DECLARED refinement the solver cannot decide against a
// value known at compile time must be REPORTED, not accepted in silence.
//
// The verdict for such a predicate arrives as
// `Unknown { reason: "SMT solver returned unknown" }`, and the arm for it
// used to be part of a `_ => {}`.  A predicate the solver cannot decide is
// not thereby true.
//
// Gradual verification is deliberate in this language, so the report is a
// WARNING rather than an error: the point is that the reader learns the
// constraint is unenforced instead of being handed a type that advertises
// a check nobody performs.
//
// THREE conditions are load-bearing, and each was learned by measuring a
// version that fired where nothing was wrong:
//
//   * the solver's verdict is `Unknown` — warning on `Valid` is nonsense;
//   * the checked value is known at compile time — the solver answers
//     `unknown` for ANY predicate over a value it does not know yet, so
//     without this the warning appears on every refinement in the stdlib,
//     `{it >= -1 && it <= 16}` included;
//   * the predicate is DECLARED, not synthesised — flow-sensitive
//     narrowing turns an `if` condition into a refinement, and warning
//     there names a constraint at a line whose author wrote none.
//
// The count is pinned at exactly ONE, and the two controls below carry the
// other half: a satisfied declared refinement and a decidable-but-violated
// one must contribute no warning at all.  A future version that reverts to
// warning broadly fails this pin rather than merely adding noise.
//
// `via_call` is the undecidable case: a predicate calling a user function.
// The tuple-projection form is deliberately NOT used here — substituting
// `it` through a projection now works, so `Int{(it, 0).0 >= 10}` is a hard
// E500 rather than an undecided one, which is the better outcome and the
// reason this file names a call instead.

fn twice(n: Int) -> Int {
    n + n
}

type ViaCall is { taps: Int{twice(it) >= 10} };

type Decidable is { taps: Int{it >= -1 && it <= 16} };

fn satisfied_stays_quiet() -> Int {
    let ok = Decidable { taps: 4 };
    ok.taps
}

fn undecided_is_reported() -> Int {
    let undecided = ViaCall { taps: 1 };
    undecided.taps
}

fn main() {
    print(satisfied_stays_quiet() + undecided_is_reported());
}
